- 5. Clinical Application & Analysis: {"5-1. clinical_dataset": "A cohort of 68 pediatric patients with relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS) from Hospital San Raffaele (HSR).", "5-2. diseases_studied": "Pediatric multiple sclerosis (MS)", "5-3. clinical_groups_n": "68", "5-4. clinical_groups_age": "mean 15.1 (SD 2.2)", "5-5. clinical_groups_sex": "F 40, M 28", "5-6. deviation_metric": "z-score map", "5-7. association_analysis": "Linear models were used to estimate correlations between GM volume z-scores and clinical variables (disease duration, EDSS, premorbid IQ) and lesion volumes (T2 LV, T1 LV).", "5-8. key_findings_brief": "Pediatric MS patients show impaired gray matter maturation, which is only partially driven by white matter inflammation, suggesting early neurodegenerative phenomena contribute to disability, while high IQ may offer protection.", "5-9. key_findings_detailed": "Pediatric patients with MS showed failures in GM development and GM atrophy progression in several cortical and subcortical regions, which were only partially related to focal WM LV. Significant correlations were found between regional GM atrophy (particularly of deep GM regions) and disability, whereas higher IQ was associated with reduced deviations from age-expected GM volumes of specific GM regions at baseline and during the follow-up.", "5-10. key_limitations": "1. Only a subset of patients had follow-up assessments. 2. Only IQ was used as a measure of reserve. 3. The clinical stability of patients limited the analysis of disability worsening prediction.", "5-11. application_notes": "The study highlights the importance of therapeutic strategies focused on neuroprotection and neurorepair in pediatric MS to prevent long-term disability."}
